---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import { roles, matrix, controls } from '../data/trust';

const counts = { Implemented: controls.filter((c) => c.state === 'Implemented').length, Partial: controls.filter((c) => c.state === 'Partial').length, Planned: controls.filter((c) => c.state === 'Planned').length };
---
<Layout title="Trust" description="How Laissez is secured today: passkeys, SSO and an authenticator app, seven roles, row-level tenant isolation, a hash-chained audit log, signed receipts, sanctions data handling and an honest SOC 2 controls map." path="trust/">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>A compliance product has to be the most careful system in the room.</h1>
      <p class="lede">This page describes what is actually built in the Laissez API and sandbox, and what is not. Laissez is pre-launch. It has no certifications, no external audit and no production customers, and this page does not pretend otherwise.</p>
      <div class="hero-cta"><a class="btn primary" href={url('app/')}>Try the sandbox</a><a class="btn" href="#controls">SOC 2 controls map</a><a class="btn" href={url('status/')}>Live status</a><a class="btn" href={url('laissez-procurement-pack.pdf')} download>Procurement pack (PDF)</a></div>
      <p class="fine">The procurement pack is one PDF for a vendor folder: company summary, architecture and data flow, security questionnaire answers in SIG Lite and CAIQ form, the controls map, roles, sub-processors, continuity, legal links and what is not yet done. It is generated from this page's data at every build.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="identity">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <h2>Identity</h2>
        <p class="lede">Nobody has a password to lose. People sign in with a passkey or through their employer's identity provider, and sessions end on their own.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cols-3">
        <div class="item"><h4>Passkeys, not passwords</h4><p>Accounts are created and used with WebAuthn passkeys only. The API checks the challenge, the origin and the relying party, and tracks the signature counter so a cloned authenticator is refused. Challenges expire and can be used once. Administrators can require passkeys that confirm the person with a PIN, fingerprint or face.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><h4>SSO through OpenID Connect</h4><p>Organizations can require sign-in through their own identity provider. The flow is authorization code with PKCE (S256), state and nonce are bound to the request, and the ID token signature is verified against the provider's published keys before anyone is signed in. Provider client secrets are sealed with AES-GCM before they are stored.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><h4>Sessions that expire</h4><p>By default a session lasts at most seven days and ends after twelve hours without activity. Administrators can shorten both, limit sign-in to approved email domains and networks, and see the rule they set summarized on every member's Security page. Only a hash of the session token is stored. People can see every active session and revoke any of them. Switching organizations issues a new session and revokes the old one.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><h4>Confirmed email</h4><p>In production, an account cannot be used until its email address is confirmed through a single-use link that expires. The confirmed address is what account recovery and security alerts go to. Changing it sends a notice to the old address and needs a new confirmation.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><h4>Recovery that a stranger cannot use</h4><p>A person who loses every passkey can recover by email. The link works once, a waiting period of 24 hours applies in production and the owner is emailed with a cancel link. With an authenticator app turned on, recovery needs its six-digit code instead of waiting, and a code cannot be reused. Recovery signs in for two hours, only to add a passkey, and signs out every other session.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><h4>Bot check and organization verification</h4><p>Sign-up sits behind a Cloudflare Turnstile check when it is configured and behind per-network rate limits always. A real organization submits its legal name, registration and country, and a person at Laissez approves it, with a sanctions screening of the name, before it can settle or hold API keys.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band light" id="authorization">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <h2>Authorization</h2>
        <p class="lede">Seven roles for people, seven scopes for API keys, and a short list of actions that no key can perform no matter what it is granted.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="scroll-hint fine">The table scrolls sideways. The first column stays in place.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="grid matrix" aria-label="Permission matrix: which of the seven roles holds each permission">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Permission</th>{roles.map((r) => <th scope="col">{r}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {matrix.map(([label, perm, cells]) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><span>{label}</span><code>{perm}</code></th>
                {cells.map((v) => <td class={v ? 'y' : 'n'}>{v ? <span class="mark" aria-hidden="true"></span> : null}<span class="sr">{v ? 'Yes' : 'No'}</span></td>)}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="cols-3 auth-notes">
        <div class="item"><h4>Scoped API keys</h4><p>A key carries only the scopes it was created with: read, orders, clients, funds, compliance, developer or admin. Keys can be limited to up to 20 IP ranges, given an expiry of up to a year, rotated so the old key is linked to the new one, and are stored only as a hash. An organization holds at most ten.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><h4>Human-only actions</h4><p>Approving a policy change and managing members or SSO are refused to every API key with a specific error, whatever its scopes. A key can propose; only a signed-in person can approve.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><h4>Two-person rule</h4><p>The person who proposes a fund policy change cannot approve it. In a sandbox you can act as a fictional teammate to try both sides alone. In a real organization that switch does not exist, so the second approval must come from a second person.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="isolation">
    <div class="wrap cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2>Tenant isolation</h2>
        <p class="lede">Isolation is enforced by the database, not by remembering to add a where clause.</p>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>Every tenant table has Postgres row-level security enabled, with a policy that compares the row's organization to the one set for the current transaction.</li>
          <li>The API's tenant connection uses a database role that cannot bypass row-level security. The role has no such privilege to misuse.</li>
          <li>Every authenticated request runs inside a transaction that first sets the organization, so a query can only ever see the rows of the organization that made the request.</li>
          <li>The audit log is insert and read only for that role. Nothing the API does can update or delete an audit event.</li>
          <li>Credential shares between two organizations are the one deliberate exception: visible to both sides of the share and to nobody else.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="code-col">
<pre class="codeblock"><code><span class="c">-- api/db/migrate-003-rls.sql, abridged</span>
<span class="k">alter table</span> decisions <span class="k">enable row level security</span>;
<span class="k">create policy</span> tenant <span class="k">on</span> decisions
  <span class="k">using</span> (workspace_id = app_ws())
  <span class="k">with check</span> (workspace_id = app_ws());

<span class="c">-- the API role: no BYPASSRLS, no superuser</span>
<span class="k">grant select, insert on</span> audit_events <span class="k">to</span> laissez_rt;

<span class="c">-- every request</span>
<span class="k">begin</span>;
<span class="k">select</span> set_config(<span class="s">'app.ws'</span>, <span class="s">$organization</span>, <span class="k">true</span>);
<span class="c">-- ... queries ...</span>
<span class="k">commit</span>;</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band raised" id="integrity">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <h2>Integrity and evidence</h2>
        <p class="lede">A regulator should be able to check a decision years later without taking Laissez's word for it. Three mechanisms, two of them live today. The verification operations are documented in the <a class="text-link" href={url('developers/reference/')}>API reference</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cols-3">
        <div class="item"><span class="k">Live</span><h4>Hash-chained audit log</h4><p>A database trigger gives every audit event a sequence number and a hash over its content and the previous event's hash, under a per-organization lock. One call recomputes every hash in one pass and reports the first broken link, if any. Exports are CSV and are themselves logged.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><span class="k">Live</span><h4>Ed25519-signed receipts</h4><p>Every decision receipt is signed over a canonical form of its fields. The public key is published, and anyone can check a receipt against it without an account. A receipt verifies from the inputs and the key alone.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><span class="k">Built, not yet scheduled</span><h4>Daily Merkle anchoring</h4><p>Once a day, the head hash of every organization's audit log becomes a leaf in one Merkle tree, and the root is written to an anchor contract on the Base Sepolia testnet. Each organization gets its proof, and a checker in the repository (<code>api/chain/verify-anchor.mjs</code>) confirms it against the contract with nothing but an RPC node, so the check does not depend on Laissez. The code, contract and checker exist and pass the local chain test; the daily job turns on once the chain deployment is live, and this page will say so when it does.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band light" id="data">
    <div class="wrap cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2>Data protection</h2>
        <p class="lede">The sandbox holds fictional people and fictional money by design. The real data Laissez keeps is small, and most of it is hashed.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>TLS everywhere, with a one-year Strict-Transport-Security header, no-referrer and nosniff on every response, and a restrictive content security policy on JSON responses.</li>
          <li>Signing keys, the encryption key and database credentials live in Cloudflare Workers secret storage. They are never in the repository or in a response.</li>
          <li>SSO client secrets and authenticator-app secrets are encrypted with AES-GCM under a dedicated key before they reach the database.</li>
          <li>Sessions, API keys, invitations, confirmation and recovery links and sign-in challenges are stored as SHA-256 hashes. A database copy does not contain a usable credential.</li>
          <li>Card and bank details never reach Laissez. Invoices are sent and paid through Stripe, and Laissez keeps only the invoice, its status and the Stripe reference.</li>
          <li>Every CSV export is escaped against spreadsheet formula injection, so a hostile client name cannot run a formula when a reviewer opens the file.</li>
          <li>Anyone signed in can download their data as JSON and delete their account without contacting Laissez. The <a class="text-link" href={url('subprocessors/')}>sub-processors</a> are listed, and a draft <a class="text-link" href={url('dpa/')}>data processing addendum</a> is published for counsel to review.</li>
          <li>Sandbox investors, teammates, funds and institutions are fictional and are deleted with the sandbox after seven days. A real account stores a name and an email address, and nothing else about a person.</li>
          <li>Product events never carry personal data: the API drops any property whose key or value looks like a name, address, email, wallet or identifier before storing the event, and keeps only a hash of the network address for rate limiting.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="compliance-data">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <h2>Compliance data</h2>
        <p class="lede">A decision is only as good as the lists and rules behind it, so both are versioned, dated and checked by machine before anyone relies on them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cols-2">
        <div class="item"><h4>Sanctions lists</h4><p>Four official lists are loaded daily: the OFAC Specially Designated Nationals list, the UN Security Council consolidated list, the EU consolidated financial sanctions list and the UK sanctions list. A load that returns fewer than half the names of the last good one is refused rather than swapped in. The status page shows each list's publisher date and age, and the sanctions component turns degraded when any list is older than 36 hours. Matching uses trigram similarity with a review threshold, so near-matches are held for a person instead of silently passed. A short list of fictional names exists for demos and is marked as such.</p></div>
        <div class="item"><h4>Rule packs</h4><p>Each jurisdiction's rules are a versioned pack with an effective-from date, an effective-to date when superseded, a summary of the change and the name of the approver. Decisions cite the pack versions they used. Every pack carries golden regression cases, at least four per jurisdiction, that run with the build; a change that flips a case fails the build. Regulator feeds from the SFC, FCA, FINMA, DFSA, ESMA, SEC and Japan's FSA are read for proposed changes, which stay drafts until counsel approves them. Counsel sign-off on launch packs is a condition of production access and is still in progress.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band raised" id="controls">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <h2>SOC 2 controls map</h2>
        <p class="lede">Laissez is not SOC 2 compliant and has not been audited. This is a map of the Trust Services Criteria that matter most for this product, with the current state of each: {counts.Implemented} implemented, {counts.Partial} partial, {counts.Planned} planned.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="grid controls">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">What exists</th><th scope="col">Gap</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {controls.map((c) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><code>{c.id}</code><span>{c.name}</span></th>
                <td><span class={`state ${c.state.toLowerCase()}`}>{c.state}</span></td>
                <td>{c.evidence}</td>
                <td class="gap">{c.gap}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="cols-2 gaps">
        <div>
          <h3>What is missing, in plain terms</h3>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>No external audit of any kind. SOC 2 Type I is planned after the first design partner is in production, Type II twelve months after that.</li>
            <li>No independent penetration test. One is a condition of opening production access.</li>
            <li>No formal incident response program: no severity scale, no on-call rotation, no written customer notification procedure.</li>
            <li>A single-region database with a six hour point-in-time history window. The restore procedure is written; the drill has not yet been run against production.</li>
            <li>No data residency options. Everything is stored in one region, and that region is not selectable.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Failure modes, as designed</h3>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>Fail closed on the way in: if a check cannot complete, subscriptions and inbound transfers do not settle.</li>
            <li>Never block the exit: redemptions stay available through any outage, subject only to sanctions screening.</li>
            <li>Settlement re-checks the decision at the moment it executes. A decision older than five minutes is not honored.</li>
            <li>Writes accept an Idempotency-Key. A retry with the same key and body gets the stored response, so a settlement cannot run twice; the same key with a different body is refused.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="fine" style="margin-top:1.5rem">Report a security concern to <span class="sel">parikshit.ambhore@rice.edu</span>. Source for every claim on this page is the API code itself; the <a class="text-link" href={url('developers/')}>developer page</a> lists the public verification endpoints.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
<style>
  .code-col { min-width: 0; }
  .table-wrap { position: relative; }
  .sr { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; margin: -1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; }
  
  .scroll-hint { display: none; margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
  .matrix { min-width: 52rem; }
  .matrix th:first-child { position: sticky; left: 0; background: var(--paper-2); z-index: 1; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .scroll-hint { display: block; } .matrix tbody th { max-width: 11rem; padding-right: 1rem; } }
  .matrix thead th { text-align: center; font-size: 0.78rem; }
  .matrix thead th:first-child { text-align: left; }
  .matrix tbody th { text-align: left; font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink); }
  .matrix tbody th span, .matrix tbody th code { display: block; }
  .matrix tbody th code { font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-weight: 400; margin-top: 0.2rem; }
  .matrix td { text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; }
  .matrix td.n { background: rgba(20, 22, 26, 0.03); }
  .mark { display: inline-block; width: 0.7rem; height: 0.7rem; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--green); box-shadow: 0 0 0 4px var(--green-wash); }
  .auth-notes { margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem); }
  .controls th[scope='row'] { font-weight: 500; min-width: 12rem; }
  .controls th[scope='row'] code, .controls th[scope='row'] span { display: block; }
  .controls th[scope='row'] code { color: var(--ink-3); font-size: 0.82rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
  .controls td { font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--ink-2); line-height: 1.55; }
  .controls td.gap { color: var(--ink-3); min-width: 14rem; }
  .controls td:nth-child(3) { min-width: 20rem; }
  .state { display: inline-block; font: 500 0.78rem/1 var(--f-ui); padding: 0.4rem 0.7rem; border-radius: 999px; white-space: nowrap; border: 1px solid transparent; }
  .state.implemented { color: var(--green); background: var(--green-wash); }
  .state.partial { color: var(--amber); background: var(--amber-wash); }
  .state.planned { color: var(--ink-2); background: var(--paper-2); }
  .gaps { margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem); }
  .gaps h3 { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .item code { font-size: 0.86em; color: var(--ink); }
</style>
